'''Education in Balisca''' is managed by the [[Secretariat of Education and Integration (Balisca)|Secretariat of Education and Integration]] (SECEI). The [[Baliscan education system|education system]] in Balisca is comprehensive, universal, and deeply integrated with the nation's [[Politics of Balisca|libertarian socialist]] and [[Communal politics in Balisca|decentralized]] principles. Characterized by its pioneering reforms, focus on equity, and high educational outcomes, Baliscan education aims to foster critical thinking, social responsibility, and universal well-being rather than solely economic output. Prior to the adoption of universal, compulsory, and secular {{W|public education}} in 1852, access to education was largely advanced by [[Islam in Balisca|Muslim]] and [[Catharism|Catharist]] communities.

The educational system is divided into {{W|preschool}} (for those under age 6), basic education (9 years, in three stages, compulsory), {{W|secondary education}} (3 years, compulsory since 1922), and higher education (subdivided in {{W|university}} and {{W|polytechnic}} education). Universities are usually organized into faculties. Institutes and schools are also common designations for autonomous subdivisions of Baliscan higher education institutions. Education in Balisca is based on {{W|cooperative learning}} and the {{W|Montessori education|Montessori method of education}}, which seeks to develop natural interests and activities rather than use formal teaching methods. During their nine years of common basic education, students are not selected, tracked, or streamed. There is also inclusive special education within the classroom and instructional efforts to minimize low achievement. There is no uniform curriculum across the Baliscan Confederation, with the {{W|libertarian municipalism|determination left}} to the individual [[Cantons of Balisca|cantons]], although some have chosen to cooperative at the [[Regions of Balisca|regional level]].

Since the adoption of the [[Iniziatiba Basica Academic Confederau|Confederal Academic Baseline Initiative]] (IBAC) in 1922, the cantons authorized the establishment of confederal-level councils to compile statistics nationwide and make recommendations for baseline standards and requirements that each region would meet to. Regions that fail to meet the standards for a consecutive two schooling periods will have their programs abrogated by their region.

